## Package comparison

The package remains aligned with approved Phase 3 and this round stays within the server/executable seam. Direct Layer owners can now await cleanup completion, and the daemon reports cleanup outcomes even when a post-bind failure is primary. The remaining package-required ownership matrix is still substantial.

## Findings

### High — The required connection-ownership matrix remains incomplete

The coder result explicitly leaves blocked command shutdown, blocked sampling shutdown, acceptance racing close, and injected connection-defect coverage outstanding. The current suite does not prove that a blocked socket command is interrupted with no late response, a blocked coordinator sample is canceled before server close returns, or every accepted socket in the shutdown race is either enrolled-and-finalized or immediately destroyed. These are central Phase 3 ownership invariants and cannot be deferred.

### High — Direct graph acquisition and complete replay are not accepted

The new direct-Layer test covers cleanup outcome delivery, but it does not count one provider Layer/source subscription, coordinator, listener, accepted/enrolled connections, and exact finalization. Existing two-client tests verify commands and one later snapshot, not that both clients each receive hello success plus immediate status and state replay before updates. Add direct graph evidence with exact counters and ordered hello/status/state/broadcast assertions.

### High — Executable signal ownership still lacks deterministic evidence

`waitForSignal` remains module-local and untested. There is no injected EventEmitter seam proving both `SIGINT` and `SIGTERM` handlers are removed on successful signal completion and interruption, nor runtime evidence that cleanup failures after a signal set nonzero process status. The static facade scan alone does not satisfy the package's executable ownership gate.

### Medium — Production connection-failure observation remains lossy

Focused hooks can synchronously observe connection causes, but production still relies on a capacity-one sliding queue. Concurrent defects may overwrite earlier causes before the logging fiber observes them. Add a bounded synchronous count/metric or equivalent and an injected write/socket-defect test proving every contained failure is observed while healthy peers and the listener remain live.

### Medium — Direct cleanup observation still requires a second runtime in the test

The direct-Layer test starts `service.awaitCleanup` with a separate `Effect.runPromise` before closing the Layer scope. This proves the Deferred can be observed externally, but not that a consumer inside the owning Effect graph can join cleanup completion without detached runtime work. Prefer an outer adapter over one supervised program or a scope-close result seam that preserves the same ownership model used in production.

## Verification

The coder reports 12 server tests, 65 coordinator/provider tests, package gates, and static scans passing. Those checks do not cover the remaining findings above.
